The old way.

10:54.020 --> 10:58.700
But the crucial detail about the CQFP is that its physical evolution is governed by something

10:58.700 --> 11:00.980
called underdamped Langevin equations.

11:01.320 --> 11:05.660
See, I promised myself I wouldn't let math terms slide by without a fight today.

11:05.660 --> 11:07.580
Underdamped Langevin equations.

11:08.000 --> 11:08.960
Break that down for me.

11:09.220 --> 11:14.340
A Langevin equation is just a way physicists model the motion of a particle when it's being

11:14.340 --> 11:16.780
subjected to random forces from its environment.

11:17.000 --> 11:17.960
Like getting bumped around.

11:18.100 --> 11:18.480
Yes.

11:18.880 --> 11:23.100
Think of a tiny pollen grain jittering around in a glass of water.

11:23.560 --> 11:26.180
It's constantly getting kicked by water molecules.

11:26.380 --> 11:26.620
Okay.

11:26.820 --> 11:29.120
That random kicking is thermal noise.

11:29.740 --> 11:32.480
The equation also accounts for friction or damping.

11:32.860 --> 11:34.200
So the resistance of the water.

11:34.320 --> 11:34.800
Exactly.

11:34.800 --> 11:40.260
Now, if a system is overdamped, it's like trying to swing a pendulum while it's submerged

11:40.260 --> 11:41.060
in thick honey.

11:41.260 --> 11:42.340
Oh, that sounds awful.

11:42.480 --> 11:43.360
It would just stop.

11:43.480 --> 11:43.860
Right.

11:44.220 --> 11:46.840
The moment you stop pushing it, it instantly stops moving.

11:46.960 --> 11:48.160
It has no meaningful inertia.

11:48.520 --> 11:50.960
But the CQFP is underdamped.

11:51.140 --> 11:51.500
Underdamped.

11:51.580 --> 11:51.680
Yeah.

11:51.680 --> 11:53.140
So it's swinging in the open air.

11:53.260 --> 11:53.500
Yes.

