need to end up trapped in the exact same bowl.

14:18.720 --> 14:23.340
They must be physically combined, completely losing the distinction of where they started.

14:23.740 --> 14:23.960
Right.

14:24.540 --> 14:31.620
Meanwhile, the skater in bowl one and the skater in bowl eleven need to basically ride the

14:31.620 --> 14:36.460
earthquake, swap bowls with each other, and settle down without ever crashing into one another.

14:36.720 --> 14:41.360
And this physical mandate, forcing one pair of states to merge while desperately trying to

14:41.360 --> 14:46.080
keep another pair of states isolated, is exactly where traditional computing architectures

14:46.080 --> 14:49.020
hit a catastrophic physical brick wall.

14:49.260 --> 14:52.680
So let's look at how we currently solve this problem in our laptops today.

14:52.980 --> 14:54.160
The standard protocol.

14:54.260 --> 14:57.540
Yeah, the standard protocol is called controlled erasure, or CE.

