15:44.800 --> 15:48.240
It is logical until you consider the rest of the landscape.

15:48.480 --> 15:48.920
Uh-oh.

15:49.100 --> 15:55.240
At the exact same time, CE looks at the O1 and 11 states and says, we must keep these

15:55.240 --> 15:55.860
two separate.

15:56.180 --> 16:02.200
So we need to maintain a massive high barrier between their two wells to ensure thermal

16:02.200 --> 16:05.740
noise doesn't accidentally bounce one state over into the wrong bowl.

16:05.740 --> 16:07.180
Oh, let me guess the problem here.

16:07.460 --> 16:09.660
It's a single connected sheet of metal.

16:09.860 --> 16:13.700
You can't just lower one wall and raise another without warping the whole thing.

16:13.740 --> 16:15.780
You're touching on the exact core issue.

16:15.860 --> 16:17.600
They share the same potential copology.

16:17.860 --> 16:17.980
Right.

16:18.320 --> 16:21.040
The landscape is a continuous, unbroken surface.

16:21.040 --> 16:25.460
The barriers are mechanically linked by the underlying physics of the circuit.

16:25.720 --> 16:27.920
Here's where it gets really interesting for me.

16:28.680 --> 16:30.320
It feels like a terrible home renovation.

16:30.520 --> 16:31.140
How so?

16:31.480 --> 16:36.020
Imagine trying to knock down a wall in your house to create a nice, open concept living

16:36.020 --> 16:36.300
room.

16:36.840 --> 16:42.260
You take a sledgehammer to it, only to realize that the exact wall you are trying to destroy

16:42.260 --> 16:46.280
is the main load-bearing pillar holding up the roof over your kitchen.

16:47.060 --> 16:48.680
That is a fantastic analogy.

16:49.160 --> 16:54.260
Controlled erasure insists on keeping every piece of data strictly defined by its position,

16:54.640 --> 16:55.100
its room.

16:55.860 --> 17:00.840
But the architecture of reality literally will not allow you to tear down one wall without

17:00.840 --> 17:03.000
compromising the structural integrity of the other.

17:03.540 --> 17:05.480
That captures the dilemma perfectly.

17:05.480 --> 17:08.760
The paper details this horrific thermodynamic trade-off.

17:08.760 --> 17:14.400
If you try to save energy by reducing the driving force that merges the 0, 0, and 10 states,

17:14.700 --> 17:18.700
you inadvertently lower the storage barrier separating 0, 1, and 11.

17:18.860 --> 17:20.620
The wall holding up the kitchen sags.

17:20.820 --> 17:21.200
Exactly.

17:21.500 --> 17:26.760
And the moment that barrier drops, your fidelity is ruined because bits start accidentally flipping

17:26.760 --> 17:28.580
due to baseline thermal vibrations.

17:29.120 --> 17:32.880
You run the risk of your computer just guessing numbers randomly because the walls are too low.

17:32.980 --> 17:33.260
Yes.

17:33.440 --> 17:37.280
So you might think, well, I'll just push the system harder and faster to get it over with.

17:37.320 --> 17:37.520
Right.

17:37.660 --> 17:38.300
Speed it up.

17:38.300 --> 17:43.940
But if you try to operate the cycle faster, the populations gain kinetic energy, the marble

17:43.940 --> 17:45.300
starts rolling too fast.

17:45.500 --> 17:49.940
When it hits the barrier that's supposed to keep it safe, it has so much momentum that

17:49.940 --> 17:53.160
it just flies right over the top, escaping confinement entirely.

17:53.540 --> 17:55.100
You literally cannot win.

17:55.480 --> 17:55.660
Right.

17:55.660 --> 17:59.360
If you go slow to save energy, the walls melt and the data bleeds.

17:59.820 --> 18:03.560
If you go fast to keep the walls high, the data jumps over the walls anyway.

18:03.560 --> 18:08.340
The paper documents a statistic here that should terrify any hardware engineer.

18:09.060 --> 18:13.720
A displacement of just a few percent between the external control parameter you are applying

18:13.720 --> 18:16.380
and the true potential minimum of the device.

18:16.600 --> 18:16.900
Wait.

18:17.120 --> 18:17.720
A few percent?

18:17.820 --> 18:18.120
Yes.

18:18.380 --> 18:22.260
That tiny shift changes the escape rate by nine orders of magnitude.

18:22.500 --> 18:22.760
Wait.

18:23.200 --> 18:24.460
I need to fully process that.

18:24.560 --> 18:24.740
Yeah.

18:24.740 --> 18:29.060
You are saying that if the machine tuning is off by maybe two or three percent, the chance

18:29.060 --> 18:32.240
of the data escaping its bowl increases by a billion times?

18:32.380 --> 18:32.640
Yes.

18:33.020 --> 18:37.120
A billion fold increase in error from a microscopic calibration drift.

18:37.200 --> 18:38.240
That is insane.

18:38.240 --> 18:43.660
Because the probability of a state escaping over a barrier depends exponentially on the height

18:43.660 --> 18:45.700
of that barrier compared to the thermal energy.

18:46.300 --> 18:51.820
A protocol that identifies a bit's identity purely with positional confinement is dangerously

18:51.820 --> 18:54.980
sensitive to the exact physical shape of that confinement.

18:55.220 --> 18:57.300
This isn't just an abstract physics problem.

18:57.620 --> 18:59.740
Why should you, listening to this, care?

19:00.380 --> 19:05.280
Because this physical tension, this unwinnable fight against the load-bearing walls of the landscape

19:05.280 --> 19:09.100
is the reason your laptop gets burning hot when you render a video.

19:09.300 --> 19:10.540
It's the primary cause.

19:10.780 --> 19:14.200
It is the reason data centers consume the electricity of small cities.

19:14.660 --> 19:17.900
It is the fundamental energy drain in our infrastructure.

19:18.660 --> 19:23.980
And the paper notes that when you force controlled erasure to operate at a specific match duration,

19:24.520 --> 19:27.640
it suffers an untenable 13 percent error rate.

19:27.640 --> 19:30.640
13 percent of your logical operations simply failing.

19:30.920 --> 19:34.060
If your bank app guessed 13 percent of your transactions wrong,

19:34.060 --> 19:35.920
society would literally collapse.

19:36.380 --> 19:38.800
And even to get that atrocious error rate,

19:39.240 --> 19:43.060
the CE protocol consumes 53.6 kilobt of work.

19:44.100 --> 19:46.740
We will dive deep into what KBT means in a few minutes,

19:46.820 --> 19:49.980
but for context, it is a unit of thermodynamic energy cost.

19:50.360 --> 19:55.680
And 53.6 is a massive, incredibly wasteful number for a single operation.

19:56.040 --> 19:57.740
The conclusion is inescapable.

19:58.140 --> 20:02.960
Controlled erasure is fundamentally broken because it refuses to let go of its safety blanket.

20:02.960 --> 20:08.120
It demands that information must always reside in a specific, measurable position,

20:08.320 --> 20:12.100
forcing the physical landscape to perform contradictory actions simultaneously.

20:12.400 --> 20:15.140
Which finally brings us to the leap of faith, the alternative.

20:15.860 --> 20:18.140
Flyxion's erasure flip protocol, or EF.

20:18.260 --> 20:18.780
The solution.

20:18.940 --> 20:19.120
Yeah.

20:19.380 --> 20:21.220
If the walls of the house are structurally flawed,

20:21.640 --> 20:24.000
this new protocol proposes something bordering on madness.

20:24.520 --> 20:27.840
It decides to tear the walls down entirely and trust the motion of the bits.

20:27.840 --> 20:31.820
The erasure flip protocol doesn't try to solve the mechanical linkage of the barriers.

20:32.100 --> 20:34.660
It simply rejects the premise that you need a barrier at all.

20:34.840 --> 20:35.140
Wow.

20:35.440 --> 20:38.300
During EF, the wall separating the O1 and 11 wells,

20:38.420 --> 20:40.440
the two states that absolutely must remain distinct,

20:40.540 --> 20:43.260
is not just lowered carefully, it is eradicated completely.

20:43.700 --> 20:44.460
Removed entirely.

20:45.160 --> 20:47.980
But wait, if you delete the barrier between the two bowls,

20:48.500 --> 20:51.680
gravity and motion are just going to mush the two states together.

20:51.900 --> 20:53.880
The files are going to corrupt each other.

20:54.060 --> 20:56.740
That is the assumption you make if you only look at position.

20:56.740 --> 20:57.260
Okay.

20:57.580 --> 21:01.660
And it's true that for roughly half of the oscillation period,

21:02.080 --> 21:06.100
the two populations pass directly through one another in the center of the landscape.

21:06.300 --> 21:06.820
Right in the middle.

21:07.100 --> 21:11.040
If you take a snapshot of their position at that exact midpoint,

21:11.520 --> 21:13.480
they are entirely indistinguishable.

21:14.060 --> 21:21.020
Both the O1 state and the 11 state occupy the exact same spatial region of the flux coordinate.

21:21.160 --> 21:22.700
So it's a literal game of chicken.

21:23.340 --> 21:25.080
Imagine those two skaters we talked about.

21:25.080 --> 21:28.480
Instead of keeping them in separate walled-off bowls,

21:29.000 --> 21:30.620
you flatten the concrete between them.

21:30.720 --> 21:31.500
Just a flat surface.

21:31.760 --> 21:35.220
You point them directly at each other and let them skate forward at top speed.

21:35.960 --> 21:38.780
For a terrifying split second in the center of the park,

21:38.960 --> 21:41.020
they are occupying the exact same space.

21:41.100 --> 21:41.520
Exactly.

21:41.800 --> 21:43.660
But because they are moving in opposite directions,

21:43.920 --> 21:45.420
they pass right by each other,

21:45.840 --> 21:47.020
basically brushing shoulders,

21:47.280 --> 21:49.520
and continue perfectly into the opposite bowls.

21:49.520 --> 21:52.940
Your skater analogy highlights the core mechanism perfectly.

21:53.660 --> 21:55.840
While the states are indistinguishable by position,

21:56.120 --> 21:59.040
they remain flawlessly distinguishable by momentum.

21:59.500 --> 22:01.340
One is going left, one is going right.

22:01.660 --> 22:03.320
One skater is moving left to right,

22:03.440 --> 22:04.820
the other is moving right to left.

22:05.420 --> 22:08.380
The identity of the data, the truth of the bit, was never lost.

22:08.520 --> 22:11.520
It was just safely stored in the speed and direction of the state,

22:11.660 --> 22:12.840
rather than its location.

22:13.020 --> 22:13.780
That is brilliant.

22:13.780 --> 22:17.480
And the genius of the protocol is that the barrier is rapidly restored

22:17.480 --> 22:21.540
at the exact moment each population reaches its destination well.

22:22.140 --> 22:24.740
I want to dig into the math of how Flyxion proves this,

22:25.060 --> 22:29.940
because the paper uses a very elegant concept to describe this handoff.

22:30.220 --> 22:32.080
They call it the carrier transformation.

22:32.560 --> 22:32.860
Right.

22:32.980 --> 22:36.400
It relies on assigning statistical weights to different types of evidence.

22:36.840 --> 22:40.980
The paper defines WQ as the weight of the evidence carried by position,

22:40.980 --> 22:44.220
and WP as the weight of the evidence carried by momentum.

22:44.880 --> 22:46.700
So in a normal, boring hard drive,

22:47.040 --> 22:51.000
WQ is basically 100%, and WP is 0%.

22:51.000 --> 22:54.700
All the evidence of the bit's identity is found by checking its parking spot.

22:55.040 --> 22:55.420
Correct.

22:55.660 --> 22:58.780
But watch what happens dynamically during the erasure flip.

22:59.480 --> 23:01.300
As the bits approach the center,

23:01.540 --> 23:04.120
the distance between them and position shrinks to 0.

23:04.980 --> 23:07.340
Consequently, WQ drops to 0.

23:07.440 --> 23:08.620
Position tells us nothing.

23:08.620 --> 23:13.020
But simultaneously, the distance between them and momentum is at its absolute maximum.

23:13.340 --> 23:15.280
So WP rises to 1.

23:15.460 --> 23:19.440
The burden of identity seamlessly transfers from the location to the trajectory.

23:19.820 --> 23:22.220
It is a fluid, continuous handoff.

23:22.980 --> 23:26.520
EF doesn't destroy the distinction and magically rebuild it from scratch.

23:26.960 --> 23:29.540
It performs a continuous carrier transformation.

23:30.180 --> 23:32.820
The evidence flows from position into momentum,

23:33.300 --> 23:36.920
and smoothly back into position as they settle into their new wells.

23:36.920 --> 23:39.740
And the performance differences when you stop fighting the architecture

23:39.740 --> 23:41.740
and start using momentum are staggering.

23:42.060 --> 23:45.120
The paper states that at a matched work cost about 30 kilobt,

23:45.820 --> 23:49.280
the erasure flip protocol is six times faster than controlled erasure.

23:49.520 --> 23:50.180
Six times.

23:50.260 --> 23:52.360
And roughly two orders of magnitude more reliable.

23:52.600 --> 23:55.080
And if you choose to match them for speed instead,

23:55.400 --> 23:57.980
allowing them to complete the gate in the same amount of time,

23:58.340 --> 24:02.560
EF's error rate drops to 650,000 times lower than CE's,

24:02.660 --> 24:04.860
while using 3.6 times less energy.

24:04.860 --> 24:07.080
That is a complete paradigm shift.

24:07.200 --> 24:07.760
It really is.

24:07.940 --> 24:10.560
650,000 times fewer errors,

24:10.880 --> 24:13.160
just by letting the bits fly through the air

24:13.160 --> 24:16.140
instead of forcing them to drag their feet along the floor.

24:16.400 --> 24:18.160
If we connect this to the bigger picture,

24:18.340 --> 24:20.840
the author makes a deeply philosophical point here.

24:21.920 --> 24:24.180
Collapse under a coarse graining map,

24:24.260 --> 24:27.100
which is a mathematical way of saying looking only at position

24:27.100 --> 24:29.040
and willfully ignoring momentum,

24:29.440 --> 24:32.140
is merely a fact about the map.

24:32.140 --> 24:34.340
It is a failure of our human observation.

24:34.720 --> 24:39.320
It is not a physical fact about the underlying distinction being destroyed.

24:39.560 --> 24:43.220
It is a failure of observation, not a failure of physics.

24:43.480 --> 24:46.240
Let me repeat that because I want that to sink in for everyone listening.

24:46.420 --> 24:46.540
Yeah.

24:46.880 --> 24:49.320
It's a failure of observation, not a failure of physics.

24:49.540 --> 24:49.740
Yes.

24:49.960 --> 24:53.700
Just because our current computer architectures are only capable of looking at position

24:53.700 --> 24:57.420
doesn't mean the universe only uses position to store reality.

24:57.420 --> 25:02.460
We have been projecting a complex multidimensional reality down onto a single flat axis

25:02.460 --> 25:06.560
and then complaining when things seem to magically disappear or crash into each other.

25:06.600 --> 25:09.940
But wait, this brings up a massive universe-sized question.

25:09.980 --> 25:10.780
Okay, what is it?

25:10.920 --> 25:15.320
If these 01 and 11 bits pass through each other perfectly like ghosts,

25:15.700 --> 25:17.960
swapping places without ever losing their identity,

25:18.500 --> 25:21.760
why does this erasure flip gate still generate heat?

25:22.060 --> 25:23.380
Ah, thermodynamics.

25:23.380 --> 25:26.360
Yeah, where is the thermodynamic cost actually coming from?

25:26.840 --> 25:31.100
Because we know any computer, no matter how clever the protocol, still gets warm.

25:31.440 --> 25:34.180
To answer that, we have to look at the ledger of the universe.

25:34.760 --> 25:37.480
We have to discuss Rolf Landauer and his principle.

25:37.640 --> 25:38.920
Okay, let's hear about Landauer.

25:39.140 --> 25:44.700
In 1961, working at IBM, Landauer published a paper that changed thermodynamics forever.

25:45.500 --> 25:48.940
Before him, people vaguely thought that the act of computing doing math,

25:49.340 --> 25:51.560
moving numbers around inherently cost energy.

25:51.560 --> 25:53.480
Like it takes physical effort to do the math.

25:53.860 --> 25:55.580
Landauer proved that was false.

25:56.040 --> 25:57.940
Computation can theoretically be free.

25:58.380 --> 25:59.740
What costs energy is forgetting.

26:00.340 --> 26:03.680
Erasing information inherently unavoidably costs energy.

26:03.900 --> 26:05.620
The universe charges a tax on forgetting.

26:05.720 --> 26:06.100
Exactly.

26:06.520 --> 26:11.360
Landauer calculated an absolute thermodynamic lower bound to erase one bit of information.

26:11.840 --> 26:14.380
For this specific partial NAND gate we are analyzing,

26:14.920 --> 26:18.760
Landauer's bound the absolute minimum energy cost demanded by the laws of physics

26:18.760 --> 26:21.140
is Sintroin 347 kilobt.

26:21.540 --> 26:24.300
Let's define those variables so we aren't just throwing letters around.

26:25.320 --> 26:25.760
KBT.

26:26.560 --> 26:30.980
The K is the Boltzmann constant, and T is the absolute temperature of the environment.

26:31.180 --> 26:35.660
Think of it as the baseline thermal jitter of the room, the background static of the universe.

26:35.660 --> 26:40.840
So 0.347 times that background jitter is the unavoidable toll we have to pay.

26:41.540 --> 26:44.560
But Flyxion's paper strictly separates where this toll is collected.

26:45.120 --> 26:50.620
It forces us to distinguish between two concepts we usually mix up, erasure and migration.

26:51.040 --> 26:54.240
We use the word erasure far too casually in everyday life.

26:54.700 --> 26:57.440
Think about the 01 and 11 states in our erasure flip.

26:57.960 --> 27:01.940
They fly toward each other, pass through each other, and trade places.

27:02.120 --> 27:02.560
A swap.

27:02.560 --> 27:04.680
Mathematically, this is called a migration.

27:05.020 --> 27:07.060
It is a bijective, one-to-one mapping.

27:07.580 --> 27:11.320
Every possible input state has exactly one unique output state.

27:11.520 --> 27:14.040
The skater from the left goes right, the skater from the right goes left.

27:14.260 --> 27:17.260
If I look at the final positions, I can easily rewind the tape in my head

27:17.260 --> 27:19.400
and know exactly where each skater started.

27:19.500 --> 27:22.200
Because it's one-to-one, it loses no logical entropy.

27:23.000 --> 27:27.800
Therefore, thermodynamically, that swap is entirely free at the logical level.

27:28.000 --> 27:32.000
It doesn't contribute a single fraction of a joule to Landauer's minimum cost.

27:32.000 --> 27:32.380
Wait, wait.

27:32.500 --> 27:36.420
So the 01 and 11 flying at high speed across the physical landscape,

27:36.620 --> 27:39.700
physically trading places, costs zero logical energy.

27:39.820 --> 27:40.140
Zero.

27:40.420 --> 27:43.900
Then where does the 0.347 KBT tax come from?

27:44.080 --> 27:46.760
It comes entirely from the other half of the NAND gate.

27:47.160 --> 27:50.900
The 0, 0, and 10 inputs, remember our earthquake machine,

27:51.400 --> 27:55.600
both of those input populations must be forced into the exact same output well,

27:55.920 --> 27:56.780
and the 10 well.

27:56.920 --> 27:57.120
Ah.

27:57.620 --> 28:00.820
Two distinct skaters being forced to occupy one single bowl.

28:00.820 --> 28:02.460
That is a genuine erasure.

28:02.800 --> 28:04.340
It is a many-to-one collapse.

28:04.660 --> 28:06.280
Once they are merged at the bottom of that bowl,

28:06.360 --> 28:08.160
you can look at the final state all you want,

28:08.240 --> 28:11.660
but you can never know whether that skater started in the 0, 0 bowl or the 10 bowl.

28:11.820 --> 28:12.760
The history is gone.

28:13.000 --> 28:14.560
The history has been permanently destroyed.

28:15.080 --> 28:19.440
This destruction of history is where the 0.347 KBT cost originates.

28:19.800 --> 28:23.000
I would love to actually walk through the Shannon entropy calculation for that,

28:23.080 --> 28:26.440
because I think seeing the math makes it so much more real.

28:26.780 --> 28:28.420
The people breaks it down step by step.

28:28.420 --> 28:29.680
It's a beautiful calculation.

28:29.940 --> 28:37.200
Let's assume our four initial states, 0, 0, DIN, 0, 1, and 11, are all equally likely to occur.

28:37.400 --> 28:37.660
Okay.

28:37.860 --> 28:40.920
So you have four possible equiprobable states.

28:41.420 --> 28:47.400
In information theory, the Shannon entropy of four equiprobable states is exactly two bits of information.

28:47.660 --> 28:49.060
Let me check my old high school Mac.

28:49.060 --> 28:52.820
The formula is the base 2 logarithm of the number of states.

28:52.960 --> 28:53.240
Correct.

28:53.700 --> 28:56.020
Log base 2 of 4 is indeed 2.

28:56.140 --> 28:56.320
Okay.

28:56.540 --> 28:56.980
I'm with you.

28:57.020 --> 28:58.300
We start with two bits of entropy.

28:58.620 --> 29:01.060
Now look at the output probabilities after the gate finishes,

29:01.360 --> 29:05.360
because the 0, 0 state and the 10 state were both forced into the 10.

29:05.400 --> 29:08.960
Well, that final 10 state now holds half of the total probability.

29:09.080 --> 29:09.260
Right.

29:09.380 --> 29:09.920
Two out of four.

29:10.060 --> 29:11.220
Its probability is 12.

29:11.220 --> 29:16.660
The 0, 1, and 11 states, which just swapped places, each maintain a probability of 14.

29:16.820 --> 29:21.540
So we have three final states with probabilities of 50%, 25%, and 25%.

29:21.540 --> 29:21.900
Yes.

29:22.140 --> 29:24.500
We plug those into the full Shannon entropy formula,

29:24.860 --> 29:29.320
which is the negative sum of each probability multiplied by its base 2 logarithm.

29:29.560 --> 29:30.440
Let's do it slowly.

29:31.240 --> 29:33.880
Negative 1 half times the log of 1 half.

29:34.140 --> 29:34.380
Okay.

29:34.580 --> 29:36.620
Minus 1 quarter times the log of 1 quarter.

29:37.360 --> 29:39.400
Minus 1 quarter times the log of 1 quarter.

29:39.400 --> 29:40.120
Oh, and you do the math.

29:40.240 --> 29:41.640
Log of 1 half is negative 1.

29:41.960 --> 29:43.440
So the first term is 0.5.

29:43.600 --> 29:43.840
Right.

29:44.000 --> 29:45.720
The log of 1 quarter is negative 2.

29:46.060 --> 29:48.180
So the next terms are 0.5 and 0.5.

29:48.320 --> 29:51.160
Wait, 0.5 plus 0.5 plus 0.5 is 1.5.

29:51.340 --> 29:53.800
The final entropy is 1.5 bits.

29:53.960 --> 29:55.260
We started with two bits of entropy.

29:55.540 --> 29:58.200
The system finishes with 1.5 bits of entropy.

29:58.660 --> 30:02.120
We have mathematically lost exactly 0.5 bits of information.

30:02.380 --> 30:05.580
And if you take that 0.5 bits and multiply it by Landauer's formula,

30:05.740 --> 30:08.420
which is KDT times the natural log of 2.

30:08.420 --> 30:11.640
You land precisely on 0.347 kilobt.

30:12.020 --> 30:13.720
That is unbelievably satisfying.

30:14.040 --> 30:17.800
We can point to the exact mathematical origin of the heat our computers generate.

30:18.220 --> 30:22.060
It has absolutely nothing to do with the physical strain of moving the bits across the board.

30:22.200 --> 30:23.880
It is the crucial revelation of the paper.

30:24.380 --> 30:26.800
Physical motion does not imply logical dissipation.

30:27.120 --> 30:28.620
Only a many-to-one identification,

30:28.940 --> 30:33.420
the irreversible destruction of past distinctions requires minimum thermodynamic dissipation.

30:34.240 --> 30:36.900
That reframes my entire understanding of reality.

30:36.900 --> 30:41.620
An irreversible operation isn't defined by stuff moving around fast and causing friction.

30:42.180 --> 30:46.980
It's defined by the universe permanently losing the ability to tell two pasts apart.

30:47.480 --> 30:48.880
It is deeply profound.

30:49.460 --> 30:52.500
However, I need to bring this back to engineering reality for a moment

30:52.500 --> 30:54.520
and explain something called work decomposition.

30:54.780 --> 30:55.300
Oh, right.

30:55.700 --> 30:59.960
If we aren't careful, we might give the impression that this computer runs on zero energy.

30:59.960 --> 31:06.240
Right, because a few minutes ago we established that a razor flip still takes between 16 and 30 kilobt of energy to run,

31:06.520 --> 31:10.700
which is way, way higher than the absolute minimum of 0.347.

31:11.240 --> 31:15.180
Where is the rest of that energy going if the swap is logically free?

31:15.440 --> 31:18.360
Flyxion breaks the total work down into three distinct buckets.

31:18.840 --> 31:25.300
Bucket one is the minimum logical work, the 0.347 kilobt we just calculated, the tax on forgetting.

31:25.460 --> 31:26.340
Bucket one, got it.

31:26.340 --> 31:29.660
Bucket two is what we call excess work, or finite time dissipation.

31:29.740 --> 31:30.440
The speed tax.

31:30.540 --> 31:36.860
Essentially, in the abstract mathematical realm, moving a state from A to B is free if you do it infinitely slowly.

31:37.100 --> 31:39.140
But we want our computers to be fast.

31:39.760 --> 31:40.240
Exactly.

31:41.040 --> 31:47.220
Accelerating and decelerating a physical magnetic flux through a real superconducting circuit creates energetic ripples.

31:47.220 --> 31:49.100
It's akin to physical friction.

31:49.540 --> 31:53.680
That's where the jump from 0.347 up to 16 kilobt comes from.

31:54.360 --> 31:57.740
Running things in finite time costs excess energy.

31:58.100 --> 31:58.680
And booket three.

31:59.020 --> 31:59.940
Architectural work.

32:00.460 --> 32:07.580
This is the energy spent by the external controller, the massive clock driving the magnetic fields that warp the concrete skate park.

32:08.140 --> 32:12.600
We can't pretend the earthquake machine is magic and runs on zero electricity.

32:12.600 --> 32:13.380
Got it.

32:13.460 --> 32:20.220
So the math checks out, the thermodynamics are sound, and we are still vastly improving on the old controlled erasure method.

32:20.620 --> 32:21.140
Vastly.

32:21.260 --> 32:27.200
But before we get into the grueling realities of actually building this, my brain is buzzing from the philosophical implications here.

32:27.880 --> 32:35.260
The idea that a distinction can hide in a different dimension, like momentum, when its primary dimension position is collapsed.

32:35.420 --> 32:36.520
It's a beautiful concept.

32:36.520 --> 32:43.060
Flickshing pulls in examples from completely different fields of science to prove this isn't just a quirk of supercooled quantum circuits.

32:43.500 --> 32:44.600
It's a universal principle.

32:44.780 --> 32:49.800
The author synthesizes incredible, diverse research to establish what they call Principle 4.

32:50.000 --> 32:50.660
Principle 4.

32:50.740 --> 32:59.460
A distinction continues through time as long as its future evolution retains a recoverable dependence on its history, even if that history is invisible right now.

32:59.700 --> 33:02.180
Let's test this principle against the real world.

33:02.180 --> 33:07.500
The paper cites a 2026 study on soil moisture by Guh and colleagues.

33:08.100 --> 33:10.720
What on earth does dirt have to do with quantum computing?

33:11.100 --> 33:15.820
It has everything to do with how we improperly measure complex systems and collapse data.

33:15.940 --> 33:16.200
Okay.

33:16.460 --> 33:19.960
The Guh study analyzed how climate scientists measure drought conditions.

33:20.220 --> 33:28.720
The standard, easy method is to take an entire vertical core of soil and average the moisture content together into one single number.

33:29.140 --> 33:30.380
They apply a coarse graining map.

33:30.380 --> 33:35.520
They project a 3D reality down to a single point, just like reading only the position of our bits.

33:35.740 --> 33:36.500
You see the connection.

33:36.920 --> 33:44.520
The inherent problem is that a soil profile that is highly mixed, say, bone dry on the top layer, but rich and wet deep down,

33:44.780 --> 33:51.340
might have the exact same average moisture number as a profile that is slightly uniformly depleted across all layers.

33:51.620 --> 33:56.600
So if you just look at the spreadsheet, those two plots of land look completely identical.

33:57.120 --> 33:59.760
The distinction between them has been erased by the measurement.

33:59.760 --> 34:03.740
But their real world survival trajectories are completely divergent.

34:03.780 --> 34:04.680
Oh, because of the roots.

34:04.940 --> 34:05.420
Exactly.

34:06.140 --> 34:12.780
If a drought hits, plants with deep tap roots will easily survive in the mixed profile because they can reach the deep water.

34:13.220 --> 34:16.740
Those same plants will die in the uniformly depleted profile.

34:16.900 --> 34:17.120
Wow.

34:17.120 --> 34:21.580
The projection, the averaging distorted reality by destroying the vertical distinction.

34:22.100 --> 34:26.660
But the system's future evolution still depended on that hidden history.

34:27.200 --> 34:32.220
The identity of the soil was safe in its vertical structure, just like the bit was safe in its momentum.

34:32.740 --> 34:33.260
Okay.

34:33.320 --> 34:35.120
What about the rat hippocampus study?

34:35.120 --> 34:37.720
Galapur et al., also from 2026.

34:37.720 --> 34:41.120
This is one of the most fascinating neuroscience papers of the decade.

34:41.860 --> 34:52.000
They discovered that rats can map the exact same physical spatial environment, the same physical maze, but simultaneously hold entirely different goal states in their minds.

34:52.000 --> 34:56.980
Like, I'm running this maze looking for the cheese versus I'm running this exact same maze looking for the water drop.

34:57.280 --> 34:57.480
Right.

34:57.720 --> 35:06.960
And for a long time, we might assume that since the spatial sensory input is identical, the spatial map in the brain's neurons would either overwrite itself or get confused.

35:07.280 --> 35:07.520
Sure.

35:07.660 --> 35:12.540
But the brain actually maintains these distinct goals in an orthogonal neural dimension.

35:12.880 --> 35:17.220
It uses a totally different axis of the neural population's firing rates.

35:17.400 --> 35:19.420
What does orthogonal mean in a physical brain?

35:19.420 --> 35:22.820
It means it operates on an independent mathematical plane.

35:23.660 --> 35:27.760
Spatial overlap in the neurons doesn't necessitate mental overlap of the goals.

35:28.340 --> 35:33.980
The distinctions coexist perfectly by occupying separable dimensions of a shared state space.

35:34.140 --> 35:44.140
Just like position and momentum, they share the exact same physical superconducting circuit, but operate on independent orthogonal axes so they don't crash into each other.

35:44.640 --> 35:45.460
It's the same math.

35:45.460 --> 35:46.080
It is.

35:46.200 --> 35:47.300
Consider the third example.

35:47.300 --> 35:51.460
Electrical double layers in batteries, researched by Lye and team.

35:51.640 --> 35:52.440
Okay, batteries.

35:52.640 --> 35:55.520
They studied what happens during rapid battery modulation.

35:56.220 --> 36:02.000
When you quickly change the voltage applied to an interface, they found that the small water molecules orient themselves almost instantly.

36:02.460 --> 36:05.160
But the larger, heavier ions lag behind.

36:05.160 --> 36:07.120
The system gets out of sync with itself.

36:07.120 --> 36:17.460
It means that if you simply read the current control setting, the voltage you are actively applying right now, it does not tell you the true internal state of the battery.

36:17.580 --> 36:18.340
Because of the lag.

36:18.340 --> 36:24.300
Because the battery's history is actively being stored in the relaxation phase of those lagging ions.

36:24.680 --> 36:35.940
Two batteries with the exact same instantaneous voltage applied to them could possess totally different internal molecular organizations entirely based on what you were doing to them five milliseconds ago.

36:35.940 --> 36:37.660
History acts as identity.

36:37.660 --> 36:42.900
If you only read the present position, the current voltage, you lose the truth.

36:43.080 --> 36:44.960
And finally, the most striking analogy.

36:45.900 --> 36:46.860
Acoustic echoes.

36:47.240 --> 36:48.500
Studied by Olgin.

36:48.500 --> 36:50.920
I love this one because it's so relatable.

36:51.540 --> 36:54.520
Think about being on a Zoom call in an empty hardwood room.

36:54.900 --> 36:57.360
Your voice echoes off the walls and it sounds terrible.

36:57.560 --> 36:57.800
Right.

36:57.960 --> 36:58.700
Lots of reverb.

36:59.060 --> 37:04.580
We spend millions of dollars developing software to aggressively scrub those multi-path echoes out of the audio, right?

37:04.800 --> 37:06.560
We literally label it noise.

37:06.920 --> 37:13.340
We call it noise because our conventional communication architectures are built to read only the direct line of sight signal.

37:13.560 --> 37:14.700
Everything else is a nuisance.

37:14.700 --> 37:25.100
But Olgin's study demonstrated that if you actually design an architecture specifically to read those secondary bouncing echoes, they carry an astonishing amount of structural information.

37:25.680 --> 37:32.540
The study achieved 96% accuracy for identity recognition solely by analyzing the echoes.

37:32.780 --> 37:38.800
The echoes bounce off the walls, the desk, the person's face, and they carry a near-perfect 3D map of the environment.

37:39.380 --> 37:44.020
The channel we were discarding as garbage was actually carrying a higher dimensional identity.

37:44.020 --> 37:44.460
Exactly.

37:44.700 --> 37:47.440
It's the exact same story as the controlled erasure protocol.

37:48.220 --> 37:51.960
To the old computing method, kinetic energy momentum is just thermal noise.

37:52.460 --> 37:56.580
It's an error source that ruins the clean position of the bit, so we try to damp it out.

37:56.660 --> 37:56.900
Mm-hmm.

37:57.300 --> 37:59.900
But to erasure flip, momentum is the echo.

38:00.240 --> 38:01.960
It's the hidden channel carrying the truth.

38:02.380 --> 38:03.820
That is a wonderful synthesis.

38:04.160 --> 38:10.040
Noise is very often just high-density information that your current architecture is too primitive to read.

38:10.040 --> 38:16.220
Okay, if momentum is this ultimate secret weapon hiding in plain sight, why don't we use it for everything?

38:16.800 --> 38:19.300
Why isn't Apple rolling out the Momentum Mac tomorrow?

38:19.300 --> 38:23.560
Because trusting momentum requires an extreme level of precision.

38:23.780 --> 38:26.560
Momentum is not a durable long-term archive.

38:27.060 --> 38:31.120
It is a highly volatile transitional bearer of information.

38:31.560 --> 38:33.620
What does that volatility look like in practice?

38:34.180 --> 38:37.720
It means momentum only functions as a memory storage.

38:37.720 --> 38:46.300
If the external controller, the master clock running the computer processor, knows exactly when to relax the confinement and exactly when to reimpose it.

38:46.700 --> 38:48.020
The timing has to be perfect.

38:48.180 --> 38:51.660
The paper defines this as the admissibility capture window.

38:52.120 --> 38:53.140
So it all comes down to timing.

38:53.580 --> 38:54.380
Razor-thin timing.

38:54.380 --> 39:00.400
If the barrier separating our two bowls rises too early, the bit hasn't finished crossing the center valley.

39:00.560 --> 39:03.580
It gets stranded partway across, hitting the rising wall.

39:03.680 --> 39:06.540
It smacks into the concrete and falls back into the wrong bowl.

39:06.880 --> 39:07.200
Yes.

39:07.480 --> 39:17.580
And conversely, if the barrier rises too late, the population reaches the other side, hits the far wall of the new bowl, bounces, and begins drifting backward into the center again.

39:17.840 --> 39:20.700
I want to use another analogy here because this paints such a vivid picture.

39:20.700 --> 39:25.120
It means momentum computing is exactly like being a trapeze artist.

39:25.400 --> 39:25.880
I like that.

39:26.120 --> 39:30.040
The distinction, the data, the flyer has to let go of the safety of the bar.

39:30.800 --> 39:34.180
For a terrifying suspended second, they have no position.

39:34.600 --> 39:35.780
They are floating in midair.

39:36.240 --> 39:45.280
The entire protocol only works if the catcher, representing the external controller, swings in at the exact perfect millisecond they're supposed to.

39:45.440 --> 39:49.900
If the catcher is late by a fraction of a second, air resistance and gravity take over.

39:49.900 --> 39:53.340
The flyer loses momentum and the memory plummets.

39:53.600 --> 39:55.160
The memory isn't just the flyer.

39:55.320 --> 39:56.640
This is wild to think about.

39:57.120 --> 40:00.700
The memory is the relationship between the flyer and the timing of the catcher.

40:01.260 --> 40:04.280
The state description of the computer actually has to be enlarged.

40:04.400 --> 40:04.840
It does.

40:05.020 --> 40:08.200
It's no longer just a list of the positions and momentums of the bits.

40:08.800 --> 40:12.620
The state must dynamically include the phase of the external controller.

40:12.780 --> 40:17.040
The clock of the computer is elevated to become a structural part of the memory itself.

40:17.040 --> 40:22.200
The author describes this complex interplay through a mechanism called holonomic reading.

40:22.700 --> 40:23.680
Okay, that's a heavyweight term.

40:24.280 --> 40:24.960
Holonomic.

40:25.420 --> 40:27.840
I'm going to guess it has something to do with holograms.

40:28.240 --> 40:30.740
Actually, it's rooted in differential geometry.

40:31.620 --> 40:37.600
A holonomy describes what happens when you transport an object around a closed loop in a curved space.

40:38.060 --> 40:45.100
And when you finally return to your exact starting point, the object you transported has been fundamentally transformed.

40:45.100 --> 40:46.680
Okay, I'm trying to picture that.

40:47.220 --> 40:49.920
Think about standing on the Earth's equator facing north.

40:50.580 --> 40:52.380
You hold an arrow pointing straight ahead.

40:53.000 --> 40:54.220
You walk to the north pole.

40:54.860 --> 40:58.400
You sidestep to the right down a different line of longitude back to the equator.

40:58.780 --> 41:00.960
And then you sidestep back to your starting point.

41:01.060 --> 41:01.380
Okay.

41:01.940 --> 41:03.380
I've walked a triangle, basically.

41:03.640 --> 41:04.800
You have walked a closed loop.

41:05.260 --> 41:08.500
But because the Earth is curved, your arrow is now pointing east.

41:08.500 --> 41:14.540
Even though I never actively rotated the arrow myself, the journey through the curved space did the rotating.

41:14.800 --> 41:15.320
Precisely.

41:15.820 --> 41:18.780
The control cycle of our quantum gate is a closed loop.

41:19.360 --> 41:25.380
The external magnetic fields pulse out, warp the landscape, and return to their exact starting values.

41:25.820 --> 41:26.560
The loop closes.

41:27.040 --> 41:30.960
But the state of the bits inside the landscape has been transformed by the journey.

41:31.360 --> 41:31.800
Exactly.

41:32.140 --> 41:35.200
And here is where Flyxion's math is truly elegant.

41:35.200 --> 41:42.340
On the storage subspace, the 0, 1, and 11-bit swapping places, it operates as an invertible permutation.

41:42.980 --> 41:44.900
A true, pure holonomy.

41:45.260 --> 41:47.200
You can walk the loop backward and undo it.

41:47.400 --> 41:49.580
The two ghost cars swap parking spots.

41:49.760 --> 41:53.140
If I want, I can just run the protocol in reverse and swap them back.

41:53.480 --> 42:00.380
But on the erasure subspace, the 0 and 10 inputs merging into 10, it operates as a non-invertible quotient.

42:00.480 --> 42:01.660
Meaning you can't undo it.

42:01.660 --> 42:05.840
The control loop closes, but two distinct histories have been collapsed into one.

42:06.200 --> 42:08.380
You can never, ever walk that loop backward.

42:09.100 --> 42:22.760
The sheer genius of the erasure flip protocol is that it executes both of these actions, a reversible geometricalonomy and an irreversible thermodynamic quotient simultaneously on orthogonal subspaces using the exact same closed control loop.

42:22.760 --> 42:25.100
That is just, it's a mechanical symphony.

42:25.400 --> 42:28.280
It's breathtaking that humans can engineer something that complex.

42:28.320 --> 42:29.220
It is quite an achievement.

42:29.500 --> 42:34.200
But let's bring this soaring theoretical symphony back down to the harsh dirt of reality.

42:34.580 --> 42:39.620
Because proving that one single trapeze artist can execute one perfect catch is amazing.

42:40.240 --> 42:44.420
But can we string a billion trapeze artists together to build a functional laptop?

42:44.420 --> 42:50.340
This is where Flyxion draws a very harsh, scientifically necessary line in the sand.

42:50.880 --> 42:54.920
The paper states clearly, universality does not equal architecture.

42:55.480 --> 42:56.980
Break that distinction down for me.

42:57.140 --> 43:00.960
We establish that a NAND gate is computationally universal.

43:01.380 --> 43:05.620
If you have one, you can theoretically express any mathematical truth table.

43:05.620 --> 43:14.860
But an architecture requires that the physical, messy output of gate A is actually physically ready to be ingested as the input of gate B.

43:15.080 --> 43:16.180
Let's test an analogy.

43:16.720 --> 43:19.320
I can build a massive, perfectly engineered water slide.

43:19.700 --> 43:22.420
And I can build a second, identical water slide right next to it.

43:22.760 --> 43:25.240
Universality says I possess the components for a water park.

43:25.340 --> 43:25.600
Okay.

43:25.840 --> 43:32.100
But architecture points out that if the end of slide A dumps the rider out going 50 miles an hour backwards and completely disoriented,

43:32.100 --> 43:35.600
they are in no condition to immediately enter slide B.

43:35.940 --> 43:37.860
That is exactly the interface problem.

43:38.220 --> 43:43.320
Direct composition of these momentum gates requires that the non-equilibrium momentum distributions,

43:43.900 --> 43:49.900
the lingering thermal vibrations, the residual flux amplitude left behind by gate A.

43:50.060 --> 43:51.180
All the splashing water.

43:51.380 --> 43:51.560
Right.

43:51.680 --> 43:58.780
All of that chaotic aftermath must somehow fall perfectly into the razor-thin admissibility window of gate B.

43:58.780 --> 44:04.120
And if it doesn't, what if the rider is just splashing around chaotically at the end of the slide?

44:04.580 --> 44:06.840
Then you are forced to pay an interface cost.

44:07.440 --> 44:13.040
You have to insert a restoration operation between every single gate to calm the system down.

44:13.740 --> 44:15.820
Mathematically, the paper calls this GN.

44:16.020 --> 44:16.260
GN.

44:16.700 --> 44:22.060
The total thermodynamic work of your computer circuit isn't just the sum of the work of the individual gates.

44:22.240 --> 44:26.660
It is the sum of the gates plus the work of all those GN interface restorations.

44:26.660 --> 44:32.740
So if you have to spend immense time and energy completely re-equilibrating the bit,

44:32.860 --> 44:36.700
calming the skater down, centering them perfectly in the bowl so they don't crash the next gate

44:36.700 --> 44:39.040
after every single erasure flip cycle,

44:39.200 --> 44:43.160
you might hemorrhage all the speed and energy advantages you just fought so hard to gain.

44:43.460 --> 44:43.980
Exactly.

44:44.400 --> 44:46.060
The overhead ruins the efficiency.

44:47.020 --> 44:51.940
Furthermore, scaling up introduces a massive fan-out problem.

44:52.100 --> 44:52.460
Fan-out.

44:52.460 --> 44:56.960
In a real computer processor, often the output of one single gate

44:56.960 --> 45:01.060
needs to feed into two or three downstream gates simultaneously.

45:01.420 --> 45:02.500
You have to copy the data.

45:02.700 --> 45:03.100
You do.

45:03.300 --> 45:08.740
But how on earth do you reliably copy a highly transient flying momentum state?

45:09.220 --> 45:13.300
By the time a signal propagates from the output of gate A down a physical wire

45:13.300 --> 45:15.420
to the inputs of gates B and C,

45:15.620 --> 45:19.660
the delicate directional distinction that carried the identity through the first gate

45:19.660 --> 45:21.640
might have completely thermalized.

45:21.640 --> 45:25.160
It's like shouting a complex password down a long, echoing hallway.

45:25.660 --> 45:31.140
If the hallway is too long, the sharp consonants of the word just blur into a low hum of thermal noise

45:31.140 --> 45:33.280
before the person at the other end can hear it.

45:33.400 --> 45:37.020
Which means any practical, scalable architecture faces a brutal choice.

45:37.560 --> 45:40.140
Do we accept that momentum can only be used internally,

45:40.440 --> 45:42.280
hidden inside each individual gate,

45:42.540 --> 45:47.040
and then we painstakingly restore the bits to boring static positions at every boundary?

45:47.240 --> 45:47.920
That sounds slow.

45:47.920 --> 45:53.440
Or do we attempt the near-impossible task of routing non-equilibrium momentum states

45:53.440 --> 45:54.800
across the entire chip,

45:55.160 --> 45:59.820
which would require inventing an entirely new class of quantum interconnect wires?

46:00.060 --> 46:03.420
I want to relate this interface cost to something you, the listener,

46:03.880 --> 46:05.640
probably deal with every week at work.

46:06.140 --> 46:09.620
You know how annoying it is when you copy text from a messy web page

46:09.620 --> 46:14.700
and you paste it into a professional email and the formatting is completely broken?

46:14.700 --> 46:17.400
Oh, the fonts are huge, the background is gray.

46:17.740 --> 46:18.580
It's a disaster.

46:18.740 --> 46:22.600
You have to undo it, right-click, and select Paste as Plain Text

46:22.600 --> 46:26.060
to strip away all the junk so it matches your email's architecture.

46:26.400 --> 46:30.000
That momentary frustration is the human equivalent of the interface cost.

46:30.100 --> 46:30.480
Exactly.

46:31.200 --> 46:35.020
If these momentum gates require too much plain text formatting cleanup

46:35.020 --> 46:37.280
between every single logical jump,

46:37.700 --> 46:39.380
the computing revolution stalls out.

46:39.380 --> 46:42.680
The energy you spend painstakingly cleaning up the bits

46:42.680 --> 46:46.640
completely destroys the energy you save by letting them fly in the first place.

46:47.020 --> 46:52.160
Flyxion is remarkably, refreshingly, intellectually honest about this limitation.

46:52.940 --> 46:57.160
The paper clearly states that it has established a universal logical primitive

46:57.160 --> 46:59.980
at the level of a single isolated gate.

47:00.140 --> 47:00.840
Just one gate.

47:01.200 --> 47:04.240
It aggressively does not claim to have solved the interface terms

47:04.240 --> 47:07.880
for a scaled-up billion-gate architecture.

47:07.880 --> 47:10.920
Which is such a rare thing to read in a modern research paper.

47:11.620 --> 47:14.300
Usually a paper finds one minor efficiency tweak

47:14.300 --> 47:17.360
and claims it will cure all the world's diseases by Tuesday.

47:17.520 --> 47:18.860
That happens quite a lot, yes.

47:19.140 --> 47:22.260
But Flyxion actually dedicates the final section of the paper

47:22.260 --> 47:25.100
to laying out exactly, explicitly,

47:25.620 --> 47:28.140
how their own theory could be proven wrong in a lab.

47:28.580 --> 47:30.800
A self-destruct button for their own life's work.

47:31.020 --> 47:35.260
A theory this revolutionary must offer a mechanism for its own falsification.

47:35.260 --> 47:38.700
If you can't build a physical test that could potentially prove it wrong,

47:38.840 --> 47:41.680
it isn't science, it's just elegant philosophy.

47:42.060 --> 47:43.220
So how do we push the button?

47:43.420 --> 47:46.040
How do we prove momentum computing is a dead end?

47:46.320 --> 47:50.240
The paper explicitly separates simulated error from physical error.

47:50.900 --> 47:53.400
Currently, all these incredible performance numbers

47:53.400 --> 47:56.680
are modeled on a computer solving stochastic differential equations

47:57.360 --> 48:00.460
under highly idealized, pristine assumptions.

48:00.820 --> 48:02.040
The classic physics joke.

48:02.040 --> 48:06.960
Assume a perfectly spherical cow in a frictionless vacuum.

48:07.460 --> 48:08.040
Quite literally.

48:08.420 --> 48:10.600
Real hardware is incredibly messy.

48:11.000 --> 48:13.500
It has colored noise, which isn't perfectly random,

48:13.780 --> 48:16.520
but has correlations that can drive systems into resonance.

48:16.980 --> 48:20.500
It has crosstalk, where the magnetic field from one wire

48:20.500 --> 48:23.080
accidentally bleeds over and twitches a neighboring bit.

48:23.420 --> 48:24.940
It has fabrication disorder,

48:25.080 --> 48:27.140
meaning when you physically manufacture the chip,

48:27.740 --> 48:29.860
the bowls in the skate park aren't perfectly shaped.

48:30.220 --> 48:31.420
Some are slightly lopsided.

48:31.420 --> 48:35.500
So how do we specifically refute the mechanism of the erasure flip?

48:35.900 --> 48:36.660
Two main avenues.

48:37.080 --> 48:38.900
First, we look at the momentum correlation time.

48:39.420 --> 48:42.440
This is a measure of how fast the bit's momentum randomizes

48:42.440 --> 48:45.080
due to unavoidable interactions with the thermal environment.

48:45.180 --> 48:46.260
Right, the background jitter.

48:46.600 --> 48:48.980
If that correlation time is significantly shorter

48:48.980 --> 48:50.680
than the duration of the flip itself,

48:51.220 --> 48:52.600
the bit dies in midair.

48:52.920 --> 48:55.200
Meaning the air resistance in the room is too high,

48:55.560 --> 48:57.800
the trapeze artist is buffeted by a windstorm,

48:57.800 --> 48:59.240
they lose all their forward speed,

48:59.400 --> 49:02.120
